window.AIBRIEFS=window.AIBRIEFS||{};Object.assign(window.AIBRIEFS,{"1960/14":{"facts":"Act 10 of Arkansas's 1958 Second Extraordinary Session required every teacher in a state-supported school or college, as a condition of employment, to file an annual affidavit listing every organization to which the teacher had belonged or regularly paid dues or contributions in the preceding five years. Arkansas teachers had no tenure: they were hired year to year and could be let go at the end of any school year without charges, notice or a hearing. B. T. Shelton, who had taught in Little Rock for twenty-five years and was a member of the NAACP, refused to file the affidavit and his contract was not renewed; Max Carr, an associate professor at the University of Arkansas, and Ernest T. Gephardt, a Little Rock high school teacher, also refused and were told they could not be re-employed.","procedure":"Shelton, with the Arkansas Teachers Association, sued in federal court, where a three-judge District Court upheld Act 10 (174 F. Supp. 351); that decision came to the Supreme Court on appeal (No. 14). Carr and Gephardt sued in state court, and the Supreme Court of Arkansas also upheld the statute (231 Ark. 641); the Court granted certiorari (No. 83) and decided the two cases together.","question":"May a State, consistently with the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment, require every public-school and state-college teacher to disclose every organization with which the teacher has been associated over the past five years, as a condition of employment?","holding":"No. Act 10 deprives teachers of their right of associational freedom protected by the Fourteenth Amendment because its unlimited and indiscriminate sweep goes far beyond what the State's legitimate interest in the fitness and competence of its teachers could justify. The judgments of both lower courts were reversed.","reasoning":"Justice Stewart began from two premises: a State plainly may investigate the competence and fitness of the teachers it hires, so the case differs from NAACP v. Alabama and Bates v. Little Rock, where the disclosure demanded bore no relevant relation to a state interest; but compelling a teacher to disclose every associational tie impairs the right of free association, a right closely allied to free speech. That impairment was aggravated because the teachers served at the absolute will of the boards receiving the affidavits, the statute did not require the information to be kept confidential, and the record showed that fear of public exposure and pressure was not groundless. The decisive point was breadth: the statute reached a teacher's church, political party and every social, professional or avocational tie, many of which could have no bearing on fitness to teach. Relying on cases such as Lovell v. Griffin, Schneider v. State, Cantwell v. Connecticut and Talley v. California, the Court held that even a legitimate and substantial governmental purpose \"cannot be pursued by means that broadly stifle fundamental personal liberties when the end can be more narrowly achieved,\" and that the breadth of a legislative abridgment must be viewed in light of less drastic means for achieving the same purpose. Measured that way, \"The unlimited and indiscriminate sweep of the statute now before us brings it within the ban of our prior cases.\"","separate":"Justice Frankfurter dissented, joined in his opinion by Justices Clark, Harlan and Whittaker. He wrote that he disliked the legislation but that it did not exceed the range of permissible state action: the careful selection of teachers depends on a comprehensive range of information, Arkansas could reasonably find the information germane, and if the affidavits were ever used to dismiss teachers solely for membership in unpopular organizations, that application could be struck down then. Justice Harlan also dissented, joined by Justices Frankfurter, Clark and Whittaker. He stressed that the cases presented no issue of racial discrimination on this record, that rights of association are not absolute, and that a requirement of this kind could not be said to exceed a State's conceded authority to determine the qualifications of its teachers.","disposition":"Reversed, 5 to 4. Opinion of the Court by Justice Stewart; Justices Frankfurter and Harlan each wrote a dissent, each joined by the other dissenters (Justices Frankfurter, Clark, Harlan and Whittaker).","model":"Claude Opus 5.5","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep364/usrep364479/usrep364479.pdf"},"1962/84":{"facts":"In 1942 Noia and two codefendants, Caminito and Bonino, were convicted in a New York court of felony murder and sentenced to life imprisonment. The only evidence against each was his signed confession. Caminito and Bonino appealed and eventually won release because their confessions were found to have been coerced in violation of the Fourteenth Amendment. Noia did not appeal, and the State later conceded that his confession was also coerced. He testified that he did not appeal partly because of the cost to his family, and his trial lawyer said he feared a retrial might bring a death sentence.","procedure":"After Noia's codefendants were freed, he sought coram nobis relief in the New York courts, which was denied because he had not appealed. The federal district court then denied habeas corpus, holding that his failure to appeal was a failure to exhaust state remedies under 28 U.S.C. § 2254. The Second Circuit reversed and ordered his release unless he was given a new trial, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a federal court grant habeas corpus relief to a state prisoner who has a conceded constitutional claim but who forfeited state review by failing to appeal in time, so that the state courts refused to reach the merits?","holding":"Yes. A state procedural default does not strip federal courts of power to grant habeas relief, and the adequate-and-independent-state-ground rule that limits Supreme Court direct review does not apply to habeas. Section 2254 covers only state remedies still open when the petition is filed. A federal judge may deny relief to a petitioner who deliberately bypassed state procedures, but only on a knowing and intelligent waiver standard, which Noia did not meet.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan traced habeas corpus from English common law and Bushell's Case to show that it has long been the remedy for any imprisonment contrary to fundamental law, including by court order. He read the Act of 1867 as extending federal habeas to its constitutional limit so that state prisoners would have a federal forum for constitutional claims. The exhaustion rule is a matter of comity that governs when the power is exercised, not whether it exists, and past decisions did not treat state-court decisions or defaults as ousting federal jurisdiction. The adequate-state-ground rule reflects limits on appellate review, while habeas lies against detention itself, and the only substantive law at issue is federal. A State's interest in enforcing its procedures is met by a limited discretion to deny relief to one who deliberately bypassed them, judged by the Johnson v. Zerbst standard of intentional relinquishment of a known right. Noia's choice, made under a real risk of the death penalty, was not such a bypass. The Court also overruled Darr v. Burford to the extent it required a petition for certiorari before habeas, and added that “Our decision today swings open no prison gates.”","separate":"Justice Clark dissented, joining Justice Harlan's opinion and adding that the ruling would bring a flood of mostly frivolous petitions, make state judgments subject to federal attack decades later, and upset federalism; he suggested legislation might be needed. Justice Harlan, joined by Justices Clark and Stewart, dissented: he argued that the decision breaks sharply with a century of precedent, that the adequate-and-independent-state-ground rule has constitutional roots and limits habeas just as it limits direct review, that the majority's waiver test is unworkable, and that New York's rule requiring a timely appeal was a reasonable ground the federal courts should respect.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was affirmed on different grounds. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court; Justices Clark, Harlan and Stewart dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep372/usrep372391/usrep372391.pdf"},"1964/52":{"facts":"The Southern Conference Educational Fund, a civil rights group working for Black citizens in the South, its executive director Dombrowski, its treasurer Smith, and its attorney Waltzer were arrested by Louisiana officers under the state's Subversive Activities and Communist Control Law and Communist Propaganda Control Law. Their offices were raided and files seized. A state judge quashed the arrest warrants and suppressed the seized evidence, but officials kept threatening prosecution. The plaintiffs alleged the threats were not meant to win convictions but to harass them and discourage civil rights work, and that the statutes were overbroad.","procedure":"The plaintiffs sued in federal court under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 for an injunction and declaratory relief. A three-judge district court dismissed the complaint, finding no irreparable injury and holding that it should abstain until state courts could narrow the statutes; the individual plaintiffs were later indicted. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"May a federal court enjoin threatened state criminal prosecutions under allegedly overbroad statutes that regulate expression, and is abstention appropriate while state courts have a chance to narrow them?","holding":"Yes. A prosecution under an overbroad statute regulating expression, brought or threatened to chill protected activity, can cause irreparable injury justifying a federal injunction, and abstention is inappropriate where a statute is attacked on its face as vague or overbroad or is applied to discourage protected activities. The challenged provisions were void on their face and could not be applied until a narrowing construction was obtained.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan acknowledged that federalism ordinarily bars federal interference with good-faith state prosecutions, since the mere possibility of an erroneous initial state ruling is not irreparable injury. But here defending a prosecution would not adequately protect First Amendment rights. Overbroad laws chill protected expression, and the “chilling effect” can come from the fact of prosecution regardless of its prospects, so that those affected should not have to risk prosecution to test their rights. The Court also relied on the allegations of arrests, seizures and threats as showing bad-faith harassment, which a state court's construction of the statute could not cure. Abstention would subject people to piecemeal criminal prosecutions that are poor vehicles for defining a vague statute, and no readily apparent narrowing construction was available. The definition of a subversive organization was as vague and broad as the one struck down in Baggett v. Bullitt, and the registration offense relied on a presumption from congressional citation of an organization as a Communist front, without the procedural safeguards required by Anti-Fascist Committee v. McGrath. The State must seek a narrowing construction in a noncriminal proceeding before it may ask to modify the injunction.","separate":"Justice Harlan, joined by Justice Clark, dissented: the decision effectively abolishes abstention in vagueness challenges to state criminal statutes involving the First Amendment, paralyzes state criminal processes, assumes without a showing that state courts will not protect federal rights, strikes the statutes without giving state courts a chance to narrow them, and lets plaintiffs avoid prosecution simply by reaching federal court first. He would have had the district court retain jurisdiction while abstaining.","disposition":"The judgment of the district court was reversed and the case remanded for a decree restraining the pending prosecutions, returning the seized papers, and barring enforcement of the provisions held void on their face. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court; Justices Black and Stewart took no part, and Justices Harlan and Clark dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep380/usrep380479/usrep380479.pdf"},"1959/64":{"facts":"The Attorney General sued on behalf of the United States under the Civil Rights Act of 1957 against members of the Board of Registrars of Terrell County, Georgia. The complaint charged that the officials, in administering their offices, used various devices to discriminate on racial grounds against Black citizens who wished to register to vote, and sought an injunction.","procedure":"The district court dismissed the complaint, holding the enforcement provision unconstitutional because its language could reach purely private action, which it thought went beyond the Fifteenth Amendment, even though the complaint alleged only official action. The government appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"May a federal court hold a statute unconstitutional in all applications because it might be read to reach private conduct beyond Congress's power, when the case before it involves only conduct that is clearly within that power?","holding":"No. A person to whom a statute may constitutionally be applied cannot challenge it on the ground that it might be unconstitutional as applied to others not before the court. Because the statute is clearly valid as applied to racial discrimination in voter registration by state officials, the complaint should not have been dismissed.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan said the power to declare acts of Congress unconstitutional rests on the duty to decide actual cases, and that courts should not anticipate constitutional questions or formulate broader rules than the facts require. The rule against challenging a law based on hypothetical applications to others is a rule of practice, with weighty reasons: courts should not consider every conceivable application of complex legislation, and a limiting construction can be given when a doubtful application actually arises. Exceptions exist, for example where a nonparty's rights would be impaired with no way to protect them, where the rule would chill speech, or in rare cases where a statute cannot be enforced in part, but none applied here. United States v. Reese was not followed to the extent it took a different approach. As applied, discrimination by state officials in the course of their duties is state action clearly within the Fifteenth Amendment, and the lack of exhausted state remedies does not change that because Congress may correct violations by every state official. Barney v. City of New York had been worn away by later decisions, and Congress may authorize the United States to sue to protect the public interest in observance of constitutional rights.","separate":"Justice Frankfurter, joined by Justice Harlan, concurred in the judgment: acts of Congress are presumptively valid, courts should apply a statute so as to save it, the measure is sustained under familiar principles, and it was unnecessary to canvass the many other opinions touching on these issues.","disposition":"The judgment of the district court was reversed. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court; Justices Frankfurter and Harlan concurred in the judgment.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep362/usrep362017/usrep362017.pdf"},"1968/413":{"facts":"Pearce was convicted in North Carolina of assault with intent to commit rape and sentenced to 12 to 15 years. Years later his conviction was reversed because an involuntary confession had been admitted, and on retrial he received eight years, which added to time already served exceeded his original sentence. Rice pleaded guilty in Alabama to four burglaries and received 10 years in total; after his convictions were set aside for lack of counsel, he was retried on three charges and sentenced to 25 years with no credit for the time he had served. Neither State offered any justification for the longer sentences.","procedure":"Each man sought federal habeas corpus. The district courts held the longer sentences unconstitutional, and the Fourth and Fifth Circuits affirmed. The Supreme Court granted certiorari in both cases.","question":"To what extent does the Constitution limit imposing a harsher sentence after a defendant has won a new trial and been convicted again, including whether time already served must be credited?","holding":"The double jeopardy guarantee against multiple punishments requires that punishment already served be fully credited against any new sentence for the same offense. Neither double jeopardy nor equal protection absolutely bars a more severe sentence on reconviction, but due process forbids vindictiveness, so reasons for a harsher sentence must appear affirmatively in the record and rest on objective information about the defendant's conduct after the original sentencing.","reasoning":"Justice Stewart relied on Ex parte Lange to hold that a person cannot be twice punished for the same offense, so time served must be subtracted from the new sentence. On the length of the sentence, the Court relied on United States v. Ball and Stroud: a defendant who has his conviction set aside may be retried, and the corollary is that any legally authorized sentence may be imposed, on the premise that the original conviction was nullified at his own request. Equal protection is not violated because a retrial yields a wholly new sentence after a wholly new trial and cannot sensibly be analyzed as an invidious classification. A judge may consider new information about the defendant's life and conduct since the first trial. But due process forbids penalizing a defendant for successfully attacking his conviction, and the fear of such retaliation could deter appeals. Because actual vindictiveness is hard to prove, the Court required that reasons for an increased sentence affirmatively appear, be based on identifiable post-sentencing conduct, and be recorded for appellate review. Neither State offered any justification, so the judgments were affirmed.","separate":"Justice Douglas, joined by Justice Marshall, concurred: double jeopardy forbids any second sentence exceeding the first, since a defendant should not have to run the gantlet of risk twice, and the interest in remaining free of double punishment outweighs the State's interest. Justice Black concurred in part and dissented in part: he agreed on credit and on rejecting the double jeopardy and equal protection claims, and would uphold Rice's result on the district court's finding of actual retaliation, but would reverse as to Pearce because the Court had no power to prescribe detailed procedural rules not found in the Constitution. Justice Harlan concurred in part and dissented in part: after Benton v. Maryland and Green v. United States, double jeopardy bars a greater punishment on retrial, though Pearce should be released only after his first sentence expires. Justice White joined the opinion except that he would allow an increased sentence based on any objective, identifiable data unknown to the judge at the first sentencing.","disposition":"The judgments of the Courts of Appeals in Nos. 413 and 418 were affirmed. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep395/usrep395711/usrep395711.pdf"},"1964/34":{"facts":"Executive Order No. 8979 (1941) set aside land in Alaska as the Kenai National Moose Range, and Public Land Order No. 487 (1948) temporarily withdrew nearby lands from settlement, location, sale or entry. Neither expressly mentioned oil and gas leasing, and the Secretary of the Interior consistently treated the lands as open to leasing, issuing leases and approving development at great expense. The Griffin lessees applied for leases between 1954 and 1955 and received them in 1958; respondents applied in August 1958 and sought the same lands, arguing the lands had been closed to leasing until a 1958 regulation.","procedure":"The Department rejected respondents' applications because the land had already been leased to earlier applicants, and the Secretary affirmed. Respondents sued in the nature of mandamus. The district court granted summary judgment for the Secretary, but the D.C. Circuit reversed, holding that the orders had withdrawn the lands from leasing so that the earlier applications and leases were nullities. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did Executive Order No. 8979 and Public Land Order No. 487 bar the Secretary of the Interior from issuing oil and gas leases on the Moose Range lands, or must a court defer to his consistent interpretation that they did not?","holding":"The orders did not bar leasing. The Secretary's long-standing interpretation that they did not is reasonable, and courts must respect an agency's reasonable interpretation of its own orders and regulations, especially where it has been publicly consistent and relied upon.","reasoning":"Chief Justice Warren noted that the Secretary had consistently read both orders as not barring leases, that this construction was a matter of public record, and that almost the entire area had been developed at great cost in reliance on it, while respondents claimed no detrimental reliance on a contrary reading. Courts show great deference to the interpretation of those charged with administering a statute, and even more so for an administrative regulation, whose administrative interpretation controls unless plainly erroneous or inconsistent with it. The Court found the language reasonably bears the Secretary's reading: settlement, location, sale and entry all concern transfer of title, so “or other disposition” could be read to cover only title-conveying dispositions, a lease does not convey title, and the orders' separate mention of leasing statutes suggests leasing was not included. The later regulations and the Secretary's discretion to refuse leases also supported his reading. The Court distinguished Wilbur as turning on the history of the Pickett Act. The fish trap exception cut slightly the other way but was a carelessly placed reassurance, not enough to make the interpretation unreasonable, and the reading of Public Land Order No. 487 followed a fortiori.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was reversed. Chief Justice Warren wrote the opinion of the Court; Justices Douglas and Harlan took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep380/usrep380001/usrep380001.pdf"},"1960/97":{"facts":"Rachel Brawner was a short-order cook for a private concessionaire running a cafeteria at the Naval Gun Factory in Washington, a restricted installation where classified weapons were developed. Her employer's contract with the Gun Factory barred employing anyone who failed its security requirements as determined by the Security Officer. The Security Officer found she did not meet them, she had to turn in her badge, and the Superintendent approved. A request for a hearing was denied as serving no useful purpose, and no specific grounds were given to her. Her employer offered her work at another restaurant, which she declined as inconvenient.","procedure":"Brawner and her union sued the Secretary of Defense and Gun Factory officers in federal district court to compel return of her badge. The district court granted summary judgment for the defendants, and the en banc Court of Appeals affirmed over four dissents. The Supreme Court granted certiorari because of an alleged conflict with Greene v. McElroy.","question":"Was the commanding officer authorized to deny Brawner access to the Gun Factory as he did, and did excluding her without notice of the grounds or a hearing deprive her of rights under the Due Process Clause of the Fifth Amendment?","holding":"Yes to authority and no to the due process claim. The Navy Regulations explicitly empowered the commanding officer to exclude her, and the Fifth Amendment did not require notice of specific grounds and a hearing in these circumstances.","reasoning":"Following Greene v. McElroy, the Court looked for explicit authority and found it in Article 0734 of the Navy Regulations, approved by the President, which let commanding officers admit tradesmen or their agents only as authorized, together with the long history of commanding officers' unquestioned power to exclude civilians from their commands. On due process, Justice Stewart stated that “The very nature of due process negates any concept of inflexible procedures universally applicable to every imaginable situation,” and that the required procedure depends on the nature of the government function and the private interest affected. Brawner's interest was not the right to follow a trade, since she remained free to work as a cook elsewhere; she lost only the opportunity to work at one military installation. The government was acting as proprietor managing the internal operation of a military establishment, like its dispatch of its own internal affairs, where it has traditionally had broad control, and like government employment that can be ended at will absent legislation. The Court assumed she could not be excluded for patently arbitrary or discriminatory reasons such as her religion or party, but here the stated reason was rational and consistent with the contract, and no badge of disloyalty or infamy was affixed that would foreclose other employment.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Black and Douglas, dissented. He doubted statutory authority but focused on due process: Brawner had a protected interest, and holding that the government's bare assertion of a valid reason ends inquiry nullifies the right not to be arbitrarily injured, since officials could use security requirements as a pretext for discrimination. She was given no process at all, and being labeled a security risk carries a stigma, so it was fundamentally unfair to remove her so summarily.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was affirmed. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court; Justices Brennan, Black and Douglas and the Chief Justice dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep367/usrep367886/usrep367886.pdf"},"1959/41":{"facts":"In 1957 Little Rock and North Little Rock, Arkansas, amended their occupational license tax ordinances to require any organization operating in the city to give the city clerk, on request, its officers, purposes, dues and contributions paid and by whom, and other information, all of which would be public. The petitioners were custodians of records for the local NAACP branches. They supplied everything except the names of members and contributors, citing the anti-NAACP climate and fear of harassment, economic reprisal, and bodily harm. The record contained uncontroverted evidence that publicly identified members had faced threats and harassment and that members had withdrawn or declined to join.","procedure":"Each petitioner was tried, convicted, and fined for violating her city's ordinance. The Arkansas Supreme Court consolidated the cases and, with two justices dissenting, upheld the convictions, calling disclosure a mere incident to a permissible legal result. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Can a city, in enforcing an occupational license tax, constitutionally compel a local NAACP branch to disclose its membership lists, consistent with the freedom of association protected against the States by the Fourteenth Amendment?","holding":"No. On this record compulsory disclosure of the membership lists would significantly interfere with members' freedom of association, and the cities showed no sufficiently compelling interest to justify it. The convictions could not stand because the petitioners could not be punished for refusing to give information the cities could not constitutionally demand.","reasoning":"Freedom of association for advancing ideas and airing grievances is protected from state invasion by the Fourteenth Amendment, including against subtle interference, and compelled disclosure of affiliation can restrain it. The record showed that exposure of members had been followed by harassment and threats, and had deterred membership, so the threat to liberty was neither speculative nor remote. Where state action significantly encroaches on personal liberty, the State must show a compelling subordinating interest. The power to tax is fundamental, but a preamble's assertion does not establish a reasonable relation between the ordinance and that purpose. The Court found no relevant correlation between the licensing power and public disclosure of membership: the NAACP's described activities were not shown to fall within any taxed occupation, no tax claim had been made against the branches, and no exemption had been sought.","separate":"Justices Black and Douglas concurred in the judgment and substantially in the opinion. They would rest on the ground that the ordinances as applied violate First Amendment freedoms of speech and assembly, which are beyond abridgment by harassment, humiliation, or exposure by government.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; Justices Black and Douglas concurred.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep361/usrep361516/usrep361516.pdf"},"1964/202":{"facts":"Griffin was tried for first-degree murder in California. He did not testify at the guilt phase, though he testified at the separate penalty phase. Under Article I, section 13 of the California Constitution, the trial court told the jury it could take his failure to explain or deny evidence within his knowledge as tending to indicate the truth of that evidence. The prosecutor also stressed in argument that the defendant knew what happened and had not taken the stand to deny or explain it.","procedure":"The jury convicted and imposed the death penalty, and the California Supreme Court affirmed before Malloy v. Hogan was decided. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to decide whether comment on a defendant's failure to testify violates the Fifth Amendment as applied to the States.","question":"Does the Fifth Amendment, applied to the States through the Fourteenth, forbid a prosecutor's comment, or a judge's instruction, that a defendant's silence at trial is evidence of guilt?","holding":"Yes. The Fifth Amendment, directly in federal court and through the Fourteenth Amendment in state court, forbids comment by the prosecution on the accused's silence and instructions by the court that such silence is evidence of guilt.","reasoning":"The Court noted that such comment would be error in a federal trial under Wilson v. United States, and that although Wilson rested on a statute, the question was whether the comment rule violates the Fifth Amendment itself. It treated the comment and the court's acquiescence as the equivalent of offering and admitting evidence from the accused's silence. Comment on refusal to testify is a remnant of the “inquisitorial system of criminal justice,” and it penalizes the exercise of a constitutional privilege by making its assertion costly. The Court rejected the argument that the inference is natural and irresistible, distinguishing what a jury may infer unaided from what it may infer when the court solemnizes silence into evidence, and noting that a defendant may stay silent for other reasons, such as fear of impeachment with prior convictions. Malloy v. Hogan requires the same standards for silence in federal and state proceedings.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred, reluctantly, because Malloy applied the Fifth Amendment to the States in all its refinements. He objected to the incorporation doctrine and said that, but for Malloy, he would not apply this non-fundamental rule to the States. Justice Stewart, joined by Justice White, dissented: he found no compulsion to testify in comment on a defendant's choice not to, saw California's cautionary instruction as a way of articulating an inference the jury would draw anyway, and viewed procedural rules of this kind as a matter for state policy.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Douglas delivered the opinion of the Court; the Chief Justice took no part. The Court reserved whether an accused may require an instruction that silence be disregarded.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep380/usrep380609/usrep380609.pdf"},"1966/79":{"facts":"In 1961, fifteen white and Black Episcopal clergymen on a prayer pilgrimage to promote racial integration planned to use the segregated waiting room and restaurant at the Jackson, Mississippi, bus terminal, and expected to be arrested. Police officers ordered them to move on, they refused, and they were arrested under a Mississippi breach-of-peace statute. All witnesses agreed the ministers entered peacefully. The police said a threatening crowd of 25 to 30 followed them, while the ministers said no crowd was present. A municipal police justice, Spencer, convicted them and imposed the maximum sentence.","procedure":"One minister received a trial de novo in county court and won a directed verdict, and the other cases were dropped. The ministers then sued the officers and the judge in federal court under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 and for common-law false arrest. A jury found for the defendants. The Fifth Circuit held the judge immune, held that the officers had a good-faith defense at common law but not under section 1983 (reading Monroe v. Pape), and remanded for a new trial on the section 1983 claim because of prejudicial cross-examination, adding that the ministers could not recover if they had gone to Mississippi expecting an illegal arrest.","question":"Does section 1983 abolish the common-law immunity of judges for judicial acts, and may police officers defend a section 1983 false-arrest claim by showing good faith and probable cause when they acted under a statute later held unconstitutional? Do plaintiffs forfeit recovery by deliberately testing their rights while expecting arrest?","holding":"A judge remains immune from section 1983 damages for acts within his judicial role, because Congress did not abolish that common-law immunity. Police officers may assert the defense of good faith and probable cause in a section 1983 action, as at common law, but the case required a new trial because the evidence on that defense was disputed. The ministers did not consent to their arrest by peacefully exercising their right to use the waiting room while expecting to be arrested.","reasoning":"Judicial immunity was long established at common law and rests on the public interest in judges deciding cases independently and without fear of suits by disappointed litigants, even when malice is alleged. The legislative record gives no clear sign that Congress meant to abolish all common-law immunities, and the Court had so held for legislators in Tenney v. Brandhove. The common law gave officers no absolute immunity, but did not hold an officer liable for arresting on probable cause, and the same reasoning supports excusing an officer who reasonably believed a statute valid. Monroe v. Pape did not decide any immunity question and read section 1983 against the background of tort law, which includes this defense. An officer need not predict later constitutional rulings, but the officers here claimed they acted to prevent violence, which turned on disputed facts, and the verdict was tainted by irrelevant cross-examination. The ministers had a right to use the waiting room and did not trick or goad the officers.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented. He read “every person” in section 1983 to include judges, pointed to the 1871 debates in which opponents warned that judges would be liable, and argued that absolute immunity is not needed to protect honest judges. He would exempt honest mistakes but not a judge who knowingly and intentionally deprives a person of civil rights, such as one who conspires with police.","disposition":"Affirmed in part, reversed in part, and remanded. Chief Justice Warren delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Douglas dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep386/usrep386547/usrep386547.pdf"},"1962/53":{"facts":"Los Angeles County narcotics officers watched a marijuana dealer, Murphy, meet George Ker in a car near an oil field, a meeting that repeated a sale Murphy had made to an undercover sergeant the night before, though the officers could not see anything pass between the men. After Ker eluded them with a U-turn, they traced his license plate to an address and learned from another officer that a previously reliable informant had said Ker bought marijuana from Murphy and sold it from his apartment. The officers went to the apartment, got a passkey from the building manager, and entered quietly without announcing themselves, fearing destruction of evidence. They saw a brick of marijuana on the kitchen sink, arrested George and Diane Ker, and then found more marijuana elsewhere in the apartment without a warrant.","procedure":"The Kers were convicted of marijuana possession and the California District Court of Appeal affirmed, finding probable cause, a lawful entry, and a valid search incident to arrest. The California Supreme Court denied review, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari as the first suitable case to apply Mapp v. Ohio.","question":"By what standard are state searches and seizures judged after Mapp v. Ohio, and under that standard was evidence seized after the officers entered the apartment unannounced and arrested the occupants admissible?","holding":"The Fourth Amendment's standard of unreasonableness applies to the States through the Fourteenth Amendment, and the same exclusionary sanction applies. Under that standard, a plurality found that the arrests rested on probable cause, that the unannounced entry was reasonable given the risk that evidence would be destroyed, and that the search incident to arrest was valid, so the convictions were affirmed.","reasoning":"Part I, for the Court, held that Mapp enforces the Fourth Amendment's own reasonableness standard against the States, but does not require rigid uniformity of working rules or disturb the primacy of trial courts' findings on reasonableness, which this Court may review independently on the record. In Parts II to V, a four-Justice plurality found probable cause for George Ker's arrest from the repeated meeting with Murphy together with the informant's corroborated tip, and for Diane Ker's arrest from the marijuana in plain view and the information that the apartment was a base for dealing. Entry without notice under California's judicial exception for exigent circumstances was not unreasonable, because the evidence could be destroyed quickly and Ker's evasive driving suggested he might expect the police. The search was incident to a lawful arrest, time was of the essence, and the brick in the kitchen was seen in plain view and was not a search. The plurality declined to reach the later search of Diane Ker's car because it was not properly raised.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred in the result, rejecting Part I's holding that state searches are judged by the Fourth Amendment's own standards and preferring Fourteenth Amendment fundamental fairness, and he found no violation under that test. Justice Brennan, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Douglas and Goldberg, joined Part I but dissented on the application: he would hold that the unannounced entry violated the Fourth Amendment, since the occupants were unaware of the officers and no activity suggested destruction of evidence, and that police experience with other suspects cannot supply the necessary showing.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Clark delivered the opinion of the Court as to Part I and announced the judgment, with Justices Black, Stewart, and White joining Parts II to V. Justice Harlan concurred in the result, and four Justices dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep374/usrep374023/usrep374023.pdf"},"1959/126":{"facts":"State officers in Oregon, acting on information that petitioners had obscene films, obtained a warrant and searched petitioner Clark's home. They found no films but seized tape and wire recordings and a recording machine believed to have been used in wiretapping. Two Oregon state courts held the search warrant and the search unlawful and ordered the items suppressed, and the state indictment was dismissed. Federal officers then obtained the articles from a bank safe-deposit box under a federal warrant, and petitioners were indicted for intercepting and divulging telephone communications and conspiracy.","procedure":"The federal district court in Oregon assumed without deciding that the state search was unreasonable but denied the motion to suppress because no federal agent had known of or taken part in it. The articles were admitted and petitioners were convicted. The Ninth Circuit affirmed on the same ground, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May articles obtained through an unreasonable search and seizure by state officers, without involvement of federal officers, be introduced over timely objection against a defendant in a federal criminal trial (the so-called silver platter doctrine)?","holding":"No. Evidence obtained by state officers in a search that would have violated the Fourth Amendment if conducted by federal officers is inadmissible over timely objection in a federal criminal trial, even without federal participation. The federal court must make its own inquiry into the reasonableness of the state search, whatever a state court has decided.","reasoning":"The Weeks rule that state-seized evidence was admissible rested on the premise that unreasonable state searches did not violate the Federal Constitution, and Wolf v. Colorado removed that premise in 1949 by holding that arbitrary police intrusion on privacy is enforceable against the States through the Fourteenth Amendment. Logic offers no basis for treating evidence taken in violation of the Fourth Amendment differently from evidence taken in violation of the Fourteenth. The Court exercised its supervisory power over federal evidence rules and weighed the exclusionary rule, whose purpose is to deter by removing the incentive to disregard the Constitution. Experience in the federal courts and in many states showed the rule had not crippled law enforcement. The old doctrine had also produced difficult case-by-case inquiries into federal participation and undercut state exclusionary policies, whereas exclusion would encourage honest federal-state cooperation. Finally, judicial integrity weighed against federal courts becoming accomplices in disobedience of the Constitution.","separate":"Justice Frankfurter dissented, arguing that the Fourth Amendment and the Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process Clause are not the same thing, doubting that the movement among the States toward exclusion was inexorable, and proposing instead that federal courts exclude such evidence where a state court enforcing its own rules had found the search unlawful and suppressed it. Justice Harlan, joined by Justices Clark and Whittaker, filed a memorandum stating that he would retain the nonexclusionary rule of Weeks and Byars, and otherwise joined Justice Frankfurter's dissent.","disposition":"Judgment of the Court of Appeals vacated and the case remanded to the district court for further proceedings, by a 5-4 vote, in an opinion by Justice Stewart.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep364/usrep364206/usrep364206.pdf"},"1959/59":{"facts":"Thompson, a long-time local resident, was in a Louisville cafe that sold food and beer, about half an hour on a Saturday evening, dancing by himself, when two officers came in on a routine check. The manager told an officer that Thompson had been there a little over half an hour and had bought nothing; Thompson said he was waiting for a bus and was arrested for loitering. Outside, the officer said Thompson was argumentative, and he was charged with disorderly conduct. The manager testified Thompson was a frequent patron who had never been told he was unwelcome, and other evidence showed he had money, a bus schedule, and a home address.","procedure":"The Louisville Police Court convicted Thompson of loitering and disorderly conduct and fined him $10 on each charge after denying his motions to dismiss for lack of evidence. Fines under $20 were not appealable in Kentucky courts, and the Kentucky Court of Appeals granted a stay so he could seek review. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does a conviction that rests on no evidence of guilt violate the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment?","holding":"Yes. The loitering and disorderly conduct convictions were totally devoid of evidentiary support, and convicting and punishing a person without evidence of guilt denies due process.","reasoning":"The Court said the question turned not on the sufficiency of the evidence but on whether the conviction rested on any evidence at all, and the city agreed that a conviction without record support would be void. Under the loitering ordinance the only possible basis was inability to give a satisfactory account of oneself while loafing on premises without the owner's consent; the evidence showed no such facts, since Thompson said he was waiting for a bus, and the manager's undisputed testimony showed implied consent. The city conceded that no law made it an offense to dance or pat one's feet in a cafe. For disorderly conduct, the only evidence was the officer's statement that Thompson was argumentative after arrest, with nothing showing disruptive conduct, and the Court assumed that merely arguing with a police officer is not disorderly conduct under Kentucky law. It likened the case to convicting on a charge not made, which is a denial of due process.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the Police Court of Louisville for further proceedings. Justice Black delivered the opinion of the Court; the text does not state a vote or any separate opinions.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep362/usrep362199/usrep362199.pdf"},"1963/402":{"facts":"Borak, who owned 2,000 shares of J. I. Case Company stock acquired before the merger, challenged Case's merger with American Tractor Corporation. He alleged that the merger was approved by a small margin through a false and misleading proxy statement, which among other things failed to disclose alleged market manipulation of American Tractor stock, and that the merger would not have been approved otherwise. He sought to void the merger and recover damages for himself and similarly situated stockholders.","procedure":"The complaint had a diversity count for breach of fiduciary duty and a count under Section 14(a) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934. The district court held that for the Section 14(a) count it could grant only declaratory relief, applied a Wisconsin security-for-expenses statute to the rest, set a $75,000 bond, and dismissed when the bond was not posted. The Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit reversed on both counts, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari limited to whether Section 27 authorizes a federal cause of action for rescission or damages.","question":"Does Section 27 of the Securities Exchange Act authorize a private federal action, including for rescission or damages, by a stockholder for a violation of Section 14(a) in a proxy solicitation for a merger?","holding":"Yes. Private parties may sue under Section 27 for violations of Section 14(a), in both derivative and direct actions, and federal courts may grant remedies beyond declaratory relief. The particular remedy awaits trial on the merits.","reasoning":"Section 27 gives district courts jurisdiction over suits to enforce any liability or duty created by the Act, and Section 14(a) aims at deceptive proxy solicitation, so its purpose of protecting investors implies judicial relief. Because injury from a deceptive proxy usually flows from harm to the corporation, excluding derivative suits would amount to denying private relief. Private enforcement supplements the Commission, which examines over 2,000 proxy statements a year and cannot independently check each. Federal courts must provide the remedies needed to make effective the congressional purpose, citing Bell v. Hood, Deckert, and Lincoln Mills. The presence of state law questions does not change the federal character of the right, and limiting federal courts to declaratory relief would push victims into state courts that might attach no liability.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Clark delivered the opinion of the Court; the text does not state a vote or any separate opinions.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep377/usrep377426/usrep377426.pdf"},"1968/12":{"facts":"Desist and others were convicted of conspiring to import and conceal heroin. An important part of the Government's evidence was tape recordings of conversations among several petitioners in a New York City hotel room, made by federal agents in the adjoining room using a microphone taped to the door on their own side, with no physical intrusion into the petitioners' room. The eavesdropping occurred before December 18, 1967, the date of Katz v. United States, and was not authorized by a warrant. Two admitted trespassory surveillance episodes were also disclosed, but the district judge found, after a hearing, that neither tainted the trial evidence.","procedure":"The district court and the Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit rejected the Fourth Amendment objection because there was no trespass or actual intrusion into a protected area, and the convictions were affirmed. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Should the rule of Katz v. United States, that electronic eavesdropping without a warrant violates the Fourth Amendment even absent physical intrusion, be applied to electronic surveillance conducted before Katz was decided, including in cases still on direct review?","holding":"No. To the extent Katz departed from earlier decisions, it applies only where the prosecution seeks to introduce the fruits of electronic surveillance conducted after December 18, 1967, so the earlier eavesdropping here did not require reversal.","reasoning":"Katz was a clear break with the past even if it was foreshadowed, because earlier cases kept applying the trespass test and had not formally overruled Olmstead and Goldman. Under Linkletter and Stovall, retroactivity turns on the purpose of the new rule, law enforcement reliance on the old rule, and the effect on the administration of justice. The purpose of extending the exclusionary rule is deterrence of police misconduct, which retroactive application would not advance because the conduct has already occurred. Officers and courts had justifiably relied on the trespass cases, including a recent Second Circuit decision on a nearly identical installation, and litigating whether past eavesdropping tainted evidence would burden courts. The same reasons undercut any distinction for cases pending on direct review, because deterrence and reliance look to the time of the search, and Mapp differs since the States already knew they were bound by the Fourth Amendment. The Court also rejected the petitioners' other challenges, agreeing with the lower courts that the disclosed surveillance had not tainted the evidence.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the judgment for the reasons in his Katz dissent, while adhering to his dissent in Linkletter. Justice Douglas dissented, objecting that giving Katz himself the new rule but denying it to Desist is not even-handed justice, so the decision was not truly wholly prospective. Justice Harlan dissented (Douglas joined Part I), arguing that all new constitutional rules must at least apply to cases pending on direct review, that the Court should vacate and remand in light of Katz, and discussing separately how retroactivity should work on habeas corpus. Justice Fortas dissented (also in Kaiser v. New York), arguing that Olmstead had long been undermined by Silverman, Clinton, and Berger, so Katz was not a clear break, and that denying a fundamental constitutional protection to those searched before Katz was unjustified and rewarded police foot-dragging.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Marshall took no part, and Justices Black (concurring), Douglas, Harlan and Fortas (dissenting) filed separate opinions.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep394/usrep394244/usrep394244.pdf"},"1962/83":{"facts":"The Philadelphia National Bank (PNB) and Girard Trust Corn Exchange Bank were the second and third largest of the 42 commercial banks headquartered in the four-county Philadelphia metropolitan area, with branches throughout it. In November 1960 their boards approved a consolidation under the PNB charter. The resulting bank would have had roughly 36 percent of the area banks' assets and deposits, and the two largest banks would have held about 59 percent of assets. The Comptroller of the Currency approved the merger even though reports from the Federal Reserve Board, the FDIC and the Attorney General all said it would substantially lessen competition.","procedure":"The United States sued in the Eastern District of Pennsylvania to enjoin the merger under § 1 of the Sherman Act and § 7 of the Clayton Act. The District Court ruled for the banks, holding among other things that § 7 did not apply to bank mergers, that the relevant market was broader than the four counties, and that competition was not likely to be substantially lessened. The United States appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does § 7 of the Clayton Act, as amended in 1950, apply to bank mergers despite the Bank Merger Act of 1960, and if so, does the proposed PNB-Girard merger violate it?","holding":"Section 7 of the Clayton Act as amended reaches bank mergers, and the Bank Merger Act of 1960 neither repealed nor immunized bank mergers from it. A merger that produces a firm with an undue share of the relevant market and significantly increases concentration is presumed likely to lessen competition substantially, and this merger (at least 30 percent share, a 33 percent rise in two-firm concentration) violates § 7.","reasoning":"Congress in 1950 meant to close a loophole by covering the whole range of corporate amalgamations from pure stock to pure asset acquisitions, so mergers fall under § 7 even though the asset provision is limited to corporations subject to the FTC; any other reading would leave a loophole in a statute designed to close one, and immunity from the antitrust laws is not lightly implied. The Bank Merger Act did not impliedly repeal § 7: it gives no express immunity, the Comptroller need not weigh competition in any particular way, and the committee reports said the antitrust laws were unaffected, so primary jurisdiction does not apply either. The relevant product market is commercial banking and the relevant geographic market is the four-county metropolitan area, where small and mid-sized customers can practically bank. Given the market share and concentration figures, the Court adopted a presumption of illegality that the banks' evidence did not rebut, treating the lay testimony of vigorous competition and available alternatives as of little weight. The banks' justifications (following customers to the suburbs, competing with large out-of-state banks for large loans, and stimulating local economic development) were rejected because de novo branching is available, countervailing power cannot justify anticompetitive effects in one market, and weighing social and economic benefits was a choice Congress had already made. Banking's regulation makes competition more, not less, important: “competition is our fundamental national economic policy.”","separate":"Justice Harlan, joined by Justice Stewart, dissented. He argued that bank mergers are a distinct field, that for ten years everyone including the Justice Department assumed the 1950 amendment did not cover them, and that Congress in the Bank Merger Act deliberately chose a regulatory public-interest approach rather than § 7, which the Court now largely nullifies. He also read the 1950 amendment and its history as aimed at industrial concentration and the asset-acquisition loophole, not bank consolidations. Justice Goldberg, in a separate memorandum, joined Harlan on the Clayton Act question but did not necessarily dissent from the judgment, since there was a substantial Sherman Act issue he did not reach. Justice White took no part.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded with direction to enter judgment enjoining the merger. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice White took no part, and Justices Harlan, Stewart and Goldberg dissented in part as described.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep374/usrep374321/usrep374321.pdf"},"1965/762":{"facts":"Cassidy and Johnson were arrested in New Jersey in January 1958 for felony murder and interrogated by police, each receiving only a warning that his statements must be voluntary and could be used against him. Each gave a recorded confession that was admitted at their joint trial after the judge found it voluntary; petitioners offered no rebuttal and their lawyers told the jury the confessions were true. They were convicted and sentenced to death, and their convictions became final about six years before this decision. In a later collateral proceeding they filed affidavits claiming they had asked for a lawyer or to contact relatives and been refused, which the state court accepted as true for the Escobedo issue.","procedure":"After repeated unsuccessful state and federal attacks on voluntariness, petitioners sought post-conviction relief in New Jersey, arguing the confessions were inadmissible under Escobedo v. Illinois. The New Jersey Supreme Court held that Escobedo did not affect convictions already final when it was decided. The Supreme Court granted certiorari and also considered whether Miranda v. Arizona, decided a week earlier, should be retroactive.","question":"Should Escobedo v. Illinois and Miranda v. Arizona be applied retroactively to defendants whose trials were completed, or whose convictions were final, before those decisions?","holding":"Escobedo applies only to trials that began after June 22, 1964, and Miranda only to trials that began after June 13, 1966. Neither is retroactive, including to cases tried earlier that were still on direct appeal.","reasoning":"Under Linkletter v. Walker and Tehan v. Shott, the Court may make a new criminal procedure rule prospective, looking to the rule's purpose, reliance on prior decisions, and the effect on the administration of justice. The choice does not depend on the importance of the constitutional right, and the Court gave retroactive effect only where a rule goes to the integrity of fact-finding, as with Gideon and Jackson v. Denno. Escobedo and Miranda mainly protect the privilege against self-incrimination and guard against unreliable statements, but in situations where the danger is less than with overt coercion. Defendants already tried can still invoke the voluntariness case law, which takes account of failure to warn or allow outside assistance. Law enforcement fairly relied on earlier cases such as Crooker and Cicenia, and retroactivity would force retrial or release of many prisoners convicted on trustworthy evidence. The same reasons support applying the rules only to trials begun after the decisions, giving officers fair notice. Petitioners' voluntariness claim was barred by an adequate state procedural ground, and their other claims lacked merit.","separate":"Justice Clark concurred in the opinion and judgment, adhering to his separate opinion in Miranda. Justices Harlan, Stewart and White concurred in the opinion and judgment but continued to believe, for reasons in their Miranda dissents, that the new rules were unjustified and unwise. Justice Black, joined by Justice Douglas, dissented, saying petitioners were entitled to the protections of the Fifth and Sixth Amendments as construed in Escobedo and Miranda, for reasons given in his dissent in Linkletter v. Walker.","disposition":"Affirmed. The opinion of the Court was by Chief Justice Warren, announced by Justice Brennan; Justices Black and Douglas dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep384/usrep384719/usrep384719.pdf"},"1959/69":{"facts":"Federal narcotics officers executing a search warrant found narcotics and paraphernalia in a bird's nest in an awning outside a window of a Washington, D.C. apartment where Jones was present, and arrested him. Jones testified that the apartment belonged to a friend, Evans, who had given him use of it and a key, that he had slept there perhaps a night, and that Evans was away in Philadelphia. The warrant rested solely on an officer's affidavit relaying an unnamed informant's report that Jones and another person sold and kept heroin in the apartment, that the informant had bought there the day before, that the informant had earlier given accurate information, that other sources gave the same information, and that Jones was known to use narcotics.","procedure":"Jones moved before trial to suppress the evidence for lack of probable cause, and the District Court denied the motion solely because he lacked standing. He was convicted on two narcotics counts and sentenced to seven years. The Court of Appeals, one judge dissenting, affirmed, agreeing he lacked standing but also holding the evidence lawful, and it rejected a new claim that the warrant was executed in violation of 18 U.S.C. § 3109. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Could Jones, who was present in a friend's apartment with permission and was charged with possessing the seized narcotics, challenge the search as a 'person aggrieved' under Rule 41(e), and was the search warrant supported by probable cause based on hearsay?","holding":"Jones had standing under Rule 41(e): possession was the basis of the charge, and he also had a sufficient interest as someone legitimately on the premises. The hearsay affidavit, supported by a substantial basis for crediting it, established probable cause. The challenge to execution of the warrant under § 3109 could not be resolved on the record and was remanded.","reasoning":"A 'person aggrieved' must be a victim of the search, but requiring a defendant charged with possession to claim ownership or possession of the property or premises puts him in a dilemma, since his allegations could help convict him or tempt him to perjury. The Government cannot prosecute for possession while arguing that the defendant had no possession at the time of the search, so possession alone gives standing. The Court also declined to import fine property-law distinctions among lessees, licensees, invitees and guests, holding that anyone legitimately on the premises where a search occurs may challenge it when its fruits are used against him, as Jones's testimony showed. On probable cause, following Draper v. United States, hearsay may support a warrant if there is a substantial basis for crediting it. Here the informant had given accurate information, other sources corroborated it, and Jones was a known user. Because the § 3109 claim turned on conflicting trial testimony about how the warrant was executed, the Court vacated and remanded for the District Court to consider it.","separate":"Justice Douglas joined the part of the opinion on standing but dissented from the holding that there was probable cause. He argued that the magistrate's duty is nondelegable, that the magistrate must know the evidence on which police propose to act and make an independent judgment, and that reliance on faceless informers makes the magistrate a tool of police interests, referring to his dissent in Draper.","disposition":"Judgment of the Court of Appeals vacated and the case remanded to the District Court. Justice Frankfurter delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Douglas dissented in part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep362/usrep362257/usrep362257.pdf"}});